[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene is centered with generous negative space, captured at a 3/4 overhead angle to clearly display the entire plate's contents. Shallow depth of field with the primary plate sharp and the second plate softly blurred in the background. Lighting is soft, natural side light from a nearby window, creating subtle shadows and a gentle sheen on the olive oil-coated nuts. The setting is a warm, aged oak wood surface with a few scattered pepitas and a stray rosemary leaf. The dish is presented on two simple, matte ceramic plates, each holding half the recipe. On each plate, a portion of sliced, crisp pear with pale, juicy flesh is arranged side-by-side with a generous mound of toasted almond and pepita mix. The almonds are golden-brown with visible toasted textures, and the pumpkin seeds are flecked with fine green specks of chopped rosemary and tiny grains of garlic powder. A light drizzle of olive oil creates a slight sheen on the nuts. The pear slices are fresh and pale, offering a contrast to the warm, toasted hues of the nuts. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating—lived-in, warm, authentic. Slight natural imperfections: a few asymmetric nut placements, a slight variation in the browning of the almonds, and scattered crumb trails. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a professional food photograph for a high-end cookbook."
]
